# arvo-strategy. Design

The strategy-marker and container-projection layer. Defines what
"strategy" means in the substrate: a compile-time tag that picks the
underlying primitive container for a given bit width. No fielded
types beyond the four ZST markers. `#![no_std]`, no alloc.

Depends on `notko` and `arvo-transparent`. Does NOT depend on
`arvo-storage`. The trait declarations are type-only and the per-N
impl bodies name only bare primitives (`u8`, `u16`, `u32`, `u64`,
`i8`..`i64`); no `Bits` reference. The `arvo-strategy` to
`arvo-storage` direction is acyclic by topology.

## Strategy markers

```rust
#[derive(ConstParamTy, ...)]
pub struct Hot;

#[derive(ConstParamTy, ...)]
pub struct Warm;

#[derive(ConstParamTy, ...)]
pub struct Cold;

#[derive(ConstParamTy, ...)]
pub struct Precise;
```

Four zero-sized types. Each derives `ConstParamTy` so it works as a
const generic argument. Each implements the sealed `Strategy` marker
trait declared here.

`Hot` selects the narrowest container per bit width. 1..=8 bits use
`u8`, 9..=16 use `u16`, 17..=32 use `u32`, 33..=64 use `u64`,
65..=128 use `u128` (single-primitive). 129..=256 dispatches via
`MultiContainer<HiT, LoT>` with the smallest-footprint pair (e.g.
129..=192 → `MultiContainer<u64, u128>`; 193..=256 →
`MultiContainer<u128, u128>`). Picked when consumers want minimum
storage and have explicit width information.

`Warm` selects the ergonomic container. Always at least `u32` for
N<=32, `u64` for 33..=64, `u128` for 65..=128. The Warm cap shifts
from 32 (round 202604271346 doc CL D2) to 128 in round 202604280500
alongside the bit-width cap extension. Wider widths (129..=256)
diagnostic the consumer at Hot or a BACKLOG-tracked Warm extension
(no native u256 means the 2x logical safety contract collapses).

`Cold` mirrors Hot's container shape; bitpacking is an access-path
concern, not a container-type concern. Column storage masks on access.
Coverage extends to 1..=256 via the Hot table plus MultiContainer.

`Precise` selects 2x-logical carriers within native primitives where
available, with saturating arithmetic. 1..=8 → `u16`, 9..=16 → `u32`,
17..=32 → `u64`, 33..=64 → `u128`. 65..=128 uses `MultiContainer<u128,
u128>` (saturating per element, post-Round-B-arith). 129..=256 awaits
the same arith bridge.

## Strategy trait

```rust
pub const trait Strategy {}
```

Sealed marker. The four markers above implement it. No methods.
Const trait so bounds compose with const fns.

## Strategy axis decomposition

Round C (round 202604280806) decomposes the bundled `Strategy` axis
into three orthogonal sub-axes, each its own sealed marker trait. Round
202605021800 lifts every axis trait to `pub const trait` so `[const]`
propagation reaches every `Self: HasAxes` projection through the
substrate.

```rust
pub const trait OverflowPolicy { /* Wrapping / Saturating */ }
pub const trait ContainerWidth { /* Min / DoubleLogical */ }
pub const trait StorageLayout { /* Dense / Bitpacked */ }
```

Each axis has two ZST markers (`Wrapping` / `Saturating`, `Min` /
`DoubleLogical`, `Dense` / `Bitpacked`). The four existing strategy
markers implement specific axis combinations:

| Marker  | OverflowPolicy | ContainerWidth | StorageLayout |
|---------|----------------|----------------|---------------|
| Hot     | Wrapping       | Min            | Dense         |
| Warm    | Wrapping       | DoubleLogical  | Dense         |
| Cold    | Wrapping       | Min            | Bitpacked     |
| Precise | Saturating     | DoubleLogical  | Dense         |

The public surface (the four markers) is unchanged. The axis traits
are the dispatch backbone: `arvo`'s arithmetic / widen / narrow
impls (`UArith`, `IArith`, `UWidenFrom`, `UNarrowFrom`) project
through `HasAxes` to pick the codegen path per axis combination.
A consumer composing `Hot + Saturating` (Hot's container width
with saturating overflow) reaches a different codegen path than
`Hot + Wrapping` does, without breaking the public marker surface.

Each axis surface carries a `const DISCRIMINANT: u8` projection.
`HasAxes::Container::DISCRIMINANT` is asserted at compile time to
match `<S as ContainerWidth>::Output::DISCRIMINANT` for every
strategy. The cross-check pins the documentation table above to
behaviour: a future round changing a strategy's axis impl without
updating `HasAxes` breaks the build, not silent runtime divergence.

`HasAxes` itself is `pub const trait` (round 202605021800). The four
strategy `impl const HasAxes for Hot/Warm/Cold/Precise` blocks lift to
`impl const`. Every consumer-side `where T: HasAxes` bound becomes
`where T: [const] HasAxes` for const-context callers.

Per the `arvo-toolbox-not-policer` workspace rule, the axis
decomposition exists so the substrate can grow its strategy lattice
without forking the marker namespace each time. Consumer-facing
expansion (new public markers) requires an explicit design round.

## Sealed marker traits and `pub const trait` discipline

Workspace-wide pattern (round 202605021800): sealed marker traits
carrying associated types or const discriminants but no method bodies
(or trivially-pure bodies) lift to `pub const trait` so `[const]`
propagation reaches every where-clause that bounds on them.

The traits covered by this round:

- `Strategy` (sealed marker; `pub const trait`).
- `OverflowPolicy`, `ContainerWidth`, `StorageLayout`, `HasAxes`
  (axis decomposition above).
- `BitsContainerFor<N, Sign>` (signed-axis projection below).
- `CrossStrategyOp<S1, S2>` (cross-strategy diagnostic marker; sibling
  of the already-const `Resolve`).
- `Signedness` (in `arvo-storage`; sealed `Unsigned` / `Signed` marker
  trait carried as a const-generic bound on `Bits<N, S, Sign>`).
- `Ieee` and `FromU8Ieee` (in `arvo`; supertrait-bound on `Identity`
  per round 202605021800 to drop the redundant `Ieee::ZERO` /
  `Ieee::ONE` declarations).
- `IntegerLike`, `FractionLike`, `BitPresentation`, `FloatLike`,
  `BoolLike` (in `arvo`; consumer-facing markers).
- `AsBool` (in `arvo-storage`; `Bool::as_bool` projection).
- `MultiContainerHalf` (in `arvo-strategy/multi_container.rs`;
  renamed from prior `BitPrim` sealing-marker to free the canonical
  name for `arvo_bits_contracts::BitPrim`).

Every existing `impl X for Y` block for these traits lifts to
`impl const X for Y` in the same round.

## Const-trait substrate bridges (`ConstEq`, `ConstOrd`, `ConstDefault`)

Round 202605021800 introduces three substrate-level const-trait
bridges. They replace stdlib non-const trait usage in const fn bodies
where the substrate composes generically.

```rust
pub const trait ConstEq {
    fn const_eq(&self, other: &Self) -> Bool;
    fn const_ne(&self, other: &Self) -> Bool {
        Bool(!self.const_eq(other).0)
    }
}

pub const trait ConstOrd: ConstEq {
    fn const_cmp(&self, other: &Self) -> Ordering;
    fn const_lt(&self, other: &Self) -> Bool;
    fn const_le(&self, other: &Self) -> Bool;
    fn const_gt(&self, other: &Self) -> Bool;
    fn const_ge(&self, other: &Self) -> Bool;
}

pub const trait ConstDefault {
    fn const_default() -> Self;
}
```

`Ordering` reuses `core::cmp::Ordering` if const-constructible on
rustc 1.96.0-nightly; otherwise the substrate ships `pub enum
ConstOrdering { Less, Equal, Greater }`. The decision is recorded at
round close.

Coverage: every primitive-wrapper substrate type (`USize`, `Cap`,
`Bool`, `Bits<N, S, Sign>`, `UFixed<I, F, S>`, `IFixed<I, F, S>`,
`FastFloat<F>`, `StrictFloat<F>`, `Mask64`, `Mask256`, `NodeId`,
`MetaCarrier`, `IBits`, `FBits`, `Width`) gains `impl const ConstEq`
and `impl const ConstDefault` in this round. `ConstOrd` lands for the
totally-ordered subset (skip `Mask` lattice; skip floats due to NaN).

Stdlib `Default`, `PartialEq`, `Eq`, `PartialOrd`, `Ord` impls remain
alongside (boundary coverage). The bridges are additive, not
replacements.

Deferred-bridge cross-references (BACKLOG-tracked, not landing in this
round):

- `ConstFrom<T>` / `ConstTryFrom<T, E>` substrate bridges. Replace
  non-const `From::from` / `TryFrom::try_from`. Round 4 / #314.
- `ConstDeref` / `ConstAsRef<T>` substrate bridges (placed in
  `arvo-transparent`). Replace non-const `Deref::deref` /
  `AsRef::as_ref`. Round 4 / #314.
- `ConstTry` / `ConstControlFlow<R, O>`. Replace non-const `Try` /
  `FromResidual`. Round 5 / #315.
- `ConstHash` / `ConstHasher` (in `arvo-hash`). Replace non-const
  `Hash::hash`. Round 5 / #315.

## Canonical typed-const surfaces: `Bounded`, `Identity`, `SignedIdentity`

`Bounded` (MIN / MAX) and `Identity` (ZERO / ONE) are the canonical
const-trait surfaces for typed-min, typed-max, typed-zero, typed-one
across the substrate. Every primitive-wrapper impls them. The trait
**is** the canonical surface: consumer code reaches for
`<T as Bounded>::MAX` / `<T as Identity>::ZERO`, or imports the
trait (`use arvo_strategy::{Bounded, Identity};`) and writes
`T::MAX` / `T::ZERO` resolved through trait associated-const
lookup.

Inherent re-definitions of the form `impl T { pub const ZERO: T =
<T as Identity>::ZERO; }` are deliberately NOT maintained. They
duplicate trait constants, promise an API consistency the substrate
would have to remember to maintain across every wrapper, and rot the
moment a trait gains a constant. The substrate ships the trait
impls; consumer ergonomics is satisfied by `use`-importing the trait.

Existing inherent constants on substrate primitives (USize::ZERO,
USize::ONE, USize::MAX, Cap::ZERO, Cap::ONE, Mask64::EMPTY,
Mask64::FULL, etc. shipped in earlier rounds) are removed during
round 202605021800's source application. Call sites that previously
read those names migrate to the trait projection. The migration is
a one-time mechanical sweep covered by the round's source
changelist.

Round 202605021800 adds `SignedIdentity` for the signed `-1`:

```rust
pub const trait SignedIdentity: Identity {
    const NEG_ONE: Self;
}
```

Per-signed-primitive `impl const SignedIdentity` for `i8`..=`i128`
and `isize`. Body wraps the underlying `-1` of the typed primitive.

Deletion: `UPrimConst` and `IPrimConst` (which previously duplicated
`Bounded` + `Identity`) are removed in this round. The two-trait-for-
one-axis pattern is rejected per `arvo-toolbox-not-policer`. The
internal references at `arith.rs:120, 148, 175, 203` rewrite to
`<<...>::T as Identity>::ZERO`. The facade re-exports of `UPrimConst`
/ `IPrimConst` from `arvo/src/strategy.rs:19-23` drop.

## MultiContainer canonical const surface (`BitPrim` + `Bounded` + `Identity`)

Round 202605021800 ships const-trait `BitPrim`, `Bounded`, and
`Identity` impls on `MultiContainer<HiT, LoT>`. The 129..=256 logical
bit-width range — the substrate's primary downstream workload — gains
a fully const-callable bit-op surface in this round.

```rust
impl<HiT: [const] BitPrim, LoT: [const] BitPrim>
const BitPrim for MultiContainer<HiT, LoT>
{
    const WIDTH: USize = HiT::WIDTH + LoT::WIDTH;
    fn count_ones(self) -> USize {
        self.hi.count_ones() + self.lo.count_ones()
    }
    fn trailing_zeros(self) -> USize {
        if self.lo.is_zero() {
            LoT::WIDTH + self.hi.trailing_zeros()
        } else {
            self.lo.trailing_zeros()
        }
    }
    // leading_zeros / bitor / bitand / bitnot / bitxor /
    // with_bit_set / with_bit_cleared / with_bit_toggled / get_bit /
    // is_zero / clear_lowest_set_bit similarly compose hi/lo halves.
}

impl<HiT: [const] Bounded, LoT: [const] Bounded>
const Bounded for MultiContainer<HiT, LoT>
{
    const MIN: Self = MultiContainer { hi: HiT::MIN, lo: LoT::MIN };
    const MAX: Self = MultiContainer { hi: HiT::MAX, lo: LoT::MAX };
}

impl<HiT: [const] Identity, LoT: [const] Identity>
const Identity for MultiContainer<HiT, LoT>
{
    const ZERO: Self = MultiContainer { hi: HiT::ZERO, lo: LoT::ZERO };
    const ONE: Self = MultiContainer { hi: HiT::ZERO, lo: LoT::ONE };
}
```

`BitPrim::WIDTH` lifts from `u16` to `USize` in this round
(MultiContainer's `WIDTH = HiT::WIDTH + LoT::WIDTH` body needs
USize-additive arithmetic). The single-half `BitPrim` impls cascade
to USize-typed `count_ones` / `trailing_zeros` / `leading_zeros`.

Naming: the prior `arvo_strategy::multi_container::BitPrim` (sealed
marker on MultiContainer halves) renames to `MultiContainerHalf` to
free the canonical name for `arvo_bits_contracts::BitPrim`. The
`arvo-storage` re-export at `lib.rs:23` switches accordingly.

## Strategy widen / narrow as `pub const trait`

Round 202605021800 lifts `UWidenFrom`, `UNarrowFrom`, `IWidenFrom`,
`INarrowFrom` from `pub trait` to `pub const trait`. All bodies are
pure (`v as $dst_ty` casts and bounded comparison branches with
`Outcome::Ok` / `Outcome::Err`). All four macro-emitted impl families
(`impl_u_widen!`, `impl_i_widen!`, `impl_u_narrow!`, `impl_i_narrow!`)
update from `impl ... for ...` to `impl const ... for ...`.

`notko::Outcome::Ok` / `Outcome::Err` constructors are const-callable
(plain enum constructors); verified to compile inside `impl const`.

This unblocks `From<UFixed<I, F, Hot>> for UFixed<I, F, Warm>` to
become `impl const ConstFrom` (Round 4 cascade); the substrate-side
widen/narrow chain is fully const-callable in this round.

## Macro-generated impls: `impl_bounded_identity_*!` extension

Round 202605021800 extends the `impl_bounded_identity_u!` macro (in
`arith.rs`) to include `usize`. The `impl_bounded_identity_i!` macro
extends to include `isize`. The macros emit `impl const Bounded` +
`impl const Identity` for the listed primitives. Bodies bottom out at
`<$ty>::MIN` / `<$ty>::MAX` / `0` / `1` for the bare primitive
(definition-site exception 3 per `no-bare-primitives.md`).

This enables `USize` and `Cap` (in `arvo-storage`) to impl `Bounded`
+ `Identity` routing through `<usize as Bounded>::MAX` etc.

## UContainerFor

```rust
pub const trait UContainerFor<const N: u8>: Strategy {
    type T: Copy + ConstParamTy_ + ...;
}

impl const UContainerFor<1> for Hot { type T = u8; }
// ... 1..=8 -> u8
impl const UContainerFor<9> for Hot { type T = u16; }
// ... 9..=16 -> u16
// ... 17..=32 -> u32
// ... 33..=64 -> u64
// ... 65..=128 -> u128 (round 202604280500)
impl const UContainerFor<129> for Hot { type T = MultiContainer<u64, u128>; }
// ... 129..=192 -> MultiContainer<u64, u128>
// ... 193..=256 -> MultiContainer<u128, u128>

impl const UContainerFor<1> for Warm { type T = u32; }
// ... 1..=8  -> u32 (was u16; now matches Precise's 2x-logical rule)
// Updated rule: round 202604280500 lifts the explicit Warm-caps-at-32
// rule to Warm-caps-at-128, with `u128` for 33..=128 and a BACKLOG-
// tracked extension above. Existing 1..=32 cells continue to match
// the round 202604271346 D2 placement.
```

Per-N impl on each of the four markers. Bands per the strategy
semantics above. Const trait so the projection
`<S as UContainerFor<N>>::T` resolves in const fn bodies. The 65..=256
range adds two new band families: native `u128` for 65..=128 and
`MultiContainer<HiT, LoT>` for 129..=256. The MultiContainer per-cell
shape lands in `## MultiContainer dispatch` below.

## IContainerFor

```rust
pub const trait IContainerFor<const N: u8>: Strategy {
    type T: Copy + ConstParamTy_ + ...;
}

impl const IContainerFor<1> for Hot { type T = i8; }
// ... matching bands per signed primitives, mirroring UContainerFor.
// 65..=128 -> i128 (single-primitive).
// 129..=256 -> MultiContainer<i_hi, i_lo> per the same strategy
//             projection rules as UContainerFor.
```

Signed counterpart. Same structure as UContainerFor; signed primitives
through `i128` and signed `MultiContainer` shapes for 129..=256.

## BitsContainerFor

```rust
pub const trait BitsContainerFor<const N: u8, Sign: Signedness>: Strategy {
    type T: Copy + ConstParamTy_ + ...;
}

impl<S, const N: u8> const BitsContainerFor<N, Unsigned> for S
where S: UContainerFor<N>
{
    type T = <S as UContainerFor<N>>::T;
}

impl<S, const N: u8> const BitsContainerFor<N, Signed> for S
where S: IContainerFor<N>
{
    type T = <S as IContainerFor<N>>::T;
}
```

Indirection trait that projects `(N, Sign)` to either the unsigned or
the signed container table. Used at the `Bits<N, S, Sign>` field
declaration site. Keeps the U / I tables independently extensible
while routing the Sign axis through a single projection trait at the
Bits surface.

UFixed and IFixed continue to bind on `UContainerFor<N>` /
`IContainerFor<N>` directly, unchanged from prior rounds. Only `Bits`
itself binds on `BitsContainerFor<N, Sign>`.

## MultiContainer dispatch

The 129..=256 logical bit-width range dispatches via
`MultiContainer<HiT, LoT>` (declared in `arvo-storage`). Strategy-driven
(HiT, LoT) projection per:

| Strategy | Projection rule | 129..=192 example | 193..=256 example |
|----------|-----------------|-------------------|-------------------|
| Hot | smallest-footprint pair | `MultiContainer<u64, u128>` | `MultiContainer<u128, u128>` |
| Cold | smallest-footprint pair (bitpacked access at column-store layer) | `MultiContainer<u64, u128>` | `MultiContainer<u128, u128>` |
| Warm | (BACKLOG: 2x-logical doubled pair; no native u256 means cap-at-128 in this round) | (unimplemented) | (unimplemented) |
| Precise | (BACKLOG: same-length doubled pair with saturating arithmetic) | (unimplemented) | (unimplemented) |

Hot and Cold share the smallest-footprint shape (the bitpacking on
Cold is access-time, not container-time). Warm and Precise extensions
into 129..=256 await the no-native-u256 design call (BACKLOG-tracked).

The cell-by-cell impl table is generated by macro across the per-N
range. Round 202604280500 lands the storage projection only; UArith /
IArith dispatch on `MultiContainer` is BACKLOG-tracked for a follow-up
round, so UFixed / IFixed at 129..=256 await that arith bridge before
their `Add` / `Sub` / `Mul` / `Div` impls compile (UFixed / IFixed cap
at 128 in this round). `Bits<129..=256, S, Sign>` is available
standalone with the storage projection landing here.

## Resolve

```rust
pub const trait Resolve<S1: Strategy, S2: Strategy>: Strategy {
    type T: Strategy;
}
```

Pairwise strategy resolution. Used when a binary op combines two
operands carrying different strategies; the result strategy is the
"broader" of the two per a documented lattice. Const trait.

## Const-trait arithmetic surface (round 202605021400)

`UArith<N>` / `IArith<N>` / `USaturating` / `ISaturating` are
`pub const trait`. Surface unchanged at the type level; the `const`
qualifier opens the trait for use in const-context call sites.

The `UPrimConst` / `IPrimConst` typed-primitive bridges shipped in
round 202605021400 are removed in round 202605021800 (replaced by the
canonical `Bounded` + `Identity` + `SignedIdentity` family above).
Generic-context bodies that previously called `<T as UPrimConst>::ZERO`
now call `<T as Identity>::ZERO`; signed-`-1` cases use
`<T as SignedIdentity>::NEG_ONE`.

## Ieee (relocated round 202605030400)

`Ieee` is the IEEE float-width seal. Relocates this round to live
alongside its supertraits `[const] Identity` and `[const] Bounded`,
both already shipping from `arvo-strategy::arith`. The previous home
at `arvo/src/float.rs` left the trait declaration one layer above
its supertraits with no need for the higher-layer placement.

Canonical signature:

```rust
pub const trait Ieee:
    sealed::Sealed + Copy + Default + PartialEq + PartialOrd
    + [const] Identity + [const] Bounded + 'static
{
    const WIDTH: u16;
}

impl const Ieee for f32 { const WIDTH: u16 = 32; }
impl const Ieee for f64 { const WIDTH: u16 = 64; }
```

Companion trait `FromU8Ieee` rides along, same orphan-rule
constraints, same logical family:

```rust
pub const trait FromU8Ieee {
    fn from_u8_ieee(n: u8) -> Self;
}

impl const FromU8Ieee for f32 { fn from_u8_ieee(n: u8) -> Self { n as f32 } }
impl const FromU8Ieee for f64 { fn from_u8_ieee(n: u8) -> Self { n as f64 } }
```

The seal admits `f32` and `f64` today. Future widenings (`f16` /
`bf16` / `f128`) extend the seal in this file plus the existing
`impl_bounded_identity_f!` macro invocation in `arvo-strategy::arith`.
The seal lives in a private `sealed` module with marker impls for the
admitted types only.

`WIDTH: u16` stays bare-primitive-typed for round 202605030400.
Round 3 (#313) lifts it to `Width` once the broader bare-`u16` to
typed `Width` substrate sweep cascades through the bridge family.

The arvo facade re-exports `Ieee` and `FromU8Ieee` from arvo-strategy
so consumer imports through `arvo::Ieee` and `arvo::FromU8Ieee` keep
working.

